// Fancy — the raster showpiece. A title that wobbles on a sine wave
// inside a pair of colour bands, over @8bitscript/raster's portable
// surface, with a frame counter that says the loop is live. It is the
// program that proves the per-scanline capability end to end — build a
// list once with raster.at(), rewrite only its value bytes every frame
// with raster.setValue() — before 2048 leans on the same surface.
//
// Four machines answer it, each as far as its chip goes:
// #fact(video.raster) is true on the C64 (a real raster interrupt
// driving a write list), on the web (the renderer applies the list at
// paint time), on the X16 (VERA's line interrupt; packages/cx16/AGENTS.md,
// "Raster splits"), and on the VIC-20 (waitFrame() applies the list, busy-
// waiting down the frame — the VIC has no interrupt). The wobble needs a
// per-line fine scroll as well, which `raster.FINE_SCROLL` says the C64,
// the web and the X16 have and the VIC-20 does not ($9000 moves the picture in
// four-pixel steps), so a VIC-20 shows the colour bands with a still
// title between them. Every raster call here sits behind one of those
// two constants, so on a PET the whole effect folds away to nothing —
// measured below — and what remains is the static title, the caption
// saying so, and the frame counter. A still screen there is the
// machine's honest answer, not a bug.
//
// ---- the band's pitch: one entry per TWO scanlines ------------------------
//
// The wobble is 12 SCROLL_X entries covering a three-text-row band with
// the title in the middle of it (picture lines 32-55), one entry every
// second scanline, not every line. That pitch is a measured C64 constraint, not a taste: the raster
// handler costs ~55 cycles to the store against a 65-cycle NTSC line,
// and a bad line leaves the CPU ~20 cycles, so a per-line cascade sets
// the compare for a line the raster is already on and the whole effect
// collapses — packages/c64/AGENTS.md's wobble-band entry records the
// solid-red capture that found this, and packages/c64/test/wobble-probe.8bs
// runs the two-line pitch for real. The web renderer is idealized and
// would take per-line entries happily; the portable program follows the
// strictest machine it runs on.
//
// ---- the stride --------------------------------------------------------------
//
// raster.setValue(entry, value) names an entry by its byte offset, and
// the byte an entry takes is the machine's own: four on the C64 (line,
// address low, address high, value), three on the web (line, slot,
// value). `raster.STRIDE` says which, and folds. Until 0.18 the portable
// surface had no such constant and this program probed for it at
// start-up (a setValue at offset 3 — entry 1 on the web, misaligned and
// refused on the C64 — with the value entry 1 already held); the probe's
// motivation is now the constant.
//
// ---- reading a screenshot -------------------------------------------------
//
//   - **The colour bands.** Background and border switch to red and
//     yellow a text row above the wobble and back to black a row below
//     it. Two flat colour edges at fixed lines are the raster list
//     applying at all — a machine whose list is dead shows one colour
//     top to bottom.
//   - **The wobble.** Inside the band, each pair of scanlines sits at
//     its own 0-7 horizontal offset from the sine table, so the title's
//     vertical strokes come out as a wave, and two captures at different
//     --frames counts show different phases. A straight title inside
//     coloured bands means the splits fire but setValue() is not landing.
//   - **FRAME must be climbing.** One per trip round the loop, same as
//     joystick: a frozen program and an idle one look different, and on
//     the static machines it is the only thing that moves.
//   - **RASTER** prints #fact(video.raster) for the machine this build
//     was made for: 1 on the C64, the web, the X16 and the VIC-20, 0 elsewhere —
//     so a still title can be read as "this machine has no per-scanline
//     hook" rather than as a broken effect.
//
// ---- what it costs --------------------------------------------------------
//
// Measured 2026-09-14, stock hardware for every target, program bytes
// then RAM for variables (`8bs build <target> --size`):
//
//   pet    1007 / 36   of a stock 4K 2001's 3071   — the tightest fit
//   vic20  1053 / 43   of an unexpanded 3583
//   c128   1051 / 37      mega65 1099 / 37      atari8 1154 / 38
//   cx16   1700 / 63      nes    40976 / 63 (fixed ROM)  — before its raster
//   c64    2040 / 54   — the one 6502 machine that pays for the wobble
//   web     95 bytes of constant data / 13
//
// The fold is exact, not approximate: on the machines where
// #fact(video.raster) is false, this file and a copy with the import and
// both `if (HAS_RASTER)` bodies deleted outright build to the SAME byte
// count (pet 1007/1007, RAM identical too, measured 2026-09-14) — the
// sine table, the probe, the list building and the per-frame rewrite are
// pruned away, not carried along inert.
//
// The X16 has answered video.raster since 2026-10-03, wobble and all:
// 5206 bytes against 3692 just before (+1514 program, +7 RAM), and x16emu
// captures at --frames 310 and 320 show the title's left edge at 284-286
// and 286-288, a different offset every two scanlines and a different
// phase each frame (packages/cx16/AGENTS.md, "Raster splits").
//
// The VIC-20 has answered video.raster since 2026-09-29 (the colour
// bands, no wobble: raster.FINE_SCROLL is false there), and pays for it:
// on the 8K release build 3654 bytes against 2803 before (+851 program,
// +24 RAM) — the frame hook, the plan builder, at() and the region probe
// (packages/vic20/AGENTS.md, "Raster splits"). The vic20 row above is the
// unexpanded build from before, when the effect folded away there; today
// fancy is 69 bytes past an unexpanded VIC-20's $1E00 ceiling and builds
// for the 8K machine only. The C64
// pays 820 bytes over its own stripped build (2040 against 1220): the
// raster list, its assembly handler and install routine, the rasterline
// translation, the sine table and the per-frame rewrite — the price only
// a machine that shows the effect pays.
//
// The C64 captures behind those numbers (2026-09-14, x64sc, NTSC at
// --frames 300 and 301, PAL at 300): the red band and its yellow border
// patches at the band's lines, the title sheared into a wave, and the
// leftmost glyph pixel measured per scanline — 154,154,155,155... at
// frame 300, 155,155,...,156 one frame later, so adjacent scanlines sit
// at different offsets AND the phase moves between frames. The web's
// C64-skin capture at --frames 300 shows the same bands, the same
// sheared title, and FRAME at exactly 00300.
import { screen } from "@8bitscript/screen";
import { text, TextColor } from "@8bitscript/text";
import { raster, Slot } from "@8bitscript/raster";
import { Video } from "@8bitscript/system";
import { audio } from "@8bitscript/audio";
import { graphics } from "@8bitscript/graphics";
import { chime } from "./chime.8ba";
import { Mark } from "./Mark.8bx";

// Whether this machine can change the picture part-way down a frame,
// and whether its raster list takes a per-line fine scroll too — the
// wobble. Both compile-time: every `if` on them below is folded while
// compiling, so a machine that answers false never sees that code.
const HAS_RASTER: bool = #fact(video.raster);
const BANDS: bool = raster.COLORS;   // this demo's colour bands: BORDER/BACKGROUND
const WOBBLES: bool = raster.FINE_SCROLL;

// This machine's row width, so cell = y * ROW + x. A bare const name is
// the only thing besides a literal that a global initializer may be, so
// every cell is worked out at run time in centered() rather than written
// as a const expression here.
const ROW: utinyint = text.COLUMNS;

// XSCROLL 0-7 over 32 steps: round(3.5 + 3.5 * sin(2 * pi * k / 32)) —
// the same table packages/c64/test/wobble-probe.8bs runs, verified on
// screen there, so the wave here is a known-good shape rather than a new
// guess.
const SINE: array<utinyint, 32> = [
    4, 4, 5, 5, 6, 6, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 6, 6, 5, 5, 4,
    3, 3, 2, 2, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3,
];

// The band: text rows 4-6 (picture lines 32-55), the title row in the
// middle. 12 entries at the two-scanline pitch cover it; with the four
// colour splits and the straightening entry the list holds 17, well
// inside the smallest raster.ENTRIES any answering machine declares (63
// on the C64).
const BAND_TOP: utinyint = 32;     // picture line: top of text row 4
const BAND_ENTRIES: utinyint = 12; // one SCROLL_X entry per two scanlines
const STRAIGHT: utinyint = 56;     // XSCROLL 0 again: first line after the band
const ABOVE: utinyint = 24;        // the colour bands open a text row above the wobble
const BELOW: utinyint = 64;        // and close a text row below it
const BAND_BACKGROUND: utinyint = 2; // red behind the title
const BAND_BORDER: utinyint = 7;     // yellow border beside it

// Where everything sits, in text rows. The title is inside the band; the
// caption, counter and fact line are below it, where the picture never
// moves.
const TITLE_ROW: utinyint = 5;
const CAPTION_ROW: utinyint = 9;
const FRAME_ROW: utinyint = 12;
const FACT_ROW: utinyint = 14;

// Trips round the main loop, wrapping at 65535 — the liveness signal,
// repainted every frame without a dirty check, exactly as joystick does.
let frames: usmallint = 0;
let frameCell: usmallint = 0;

// The wobble's walk through the sine table, and the two probed numbers
// the per-frame rewrite needs: bytes per entry in setValue's unit, and
// the byte offset of the band's first SCROLL_X entry (index 2 — the two
// opening colour splits come first). All three are only ever touched
// inside `if (HAS_RASTER)`.
let phase: utinyint = 0;
let bandFirst: utinyint = 0;

// The cell where a `width`-cell line starts centred on `row` — centred
// the way joystick centres its 22-column map, so the narrowest machine
// (the VIC-20's 22 columns) fits everything printed here. Widened
// through a usmallint before the multiply: a binop computes at its
// operands' own width, and row * ROW overflows a byte on an 80-column
// machine (packages/c64/AGENTS.md records the sprite bug this exact
// shape caused).
function centered(row: utinyint, width: utinyint): usmallint {
    let col: utinyint = 0;
    if (text.COLUMNS > width) {
        col = (text.COLUMNS - width) >> 1;
    }
    let cell: usmallint = row;
    cell = cell * ROW + col;
    return cell;
}

// The static screen, drawn once before the loop starts. The only cell
// the loop touches afterwards is the frame counter. text.setColor is a
// deliberately empty function on the machines with no per-cell colour
// and the compiler deletes the call, so it is made unconditionally, the
// same as joystick.
function drawScreen(): void {
    text.setColor(TextColor.YELLOW);
    text.print(centered(TITLE_ROW, 9), "F A N C Y");
    text.setColor(TextColor.WHITE);

    // HAS_RASTER is a compile-time constant, so only one of these two
    // captions is in the build at all.
    if (WOBBLES) {
        text.print(centered(CAPTION_ROW, 17), "THE TITLE WOBBLES");
    } else if (BANDS) {
        text.print(centered(CAPTION_ROW, 18), "SPLITS, NO WOBBLE");
    } else if (HAS_RASTER) {
        // A real raster capability this demo doesn't draw with: the PET's
        // is a character-set split (Slot.CHARSET), not a colour one, and
        // this showpiece is specifically the colour one.
        text.print(centered(CAPTION_ROW, 19), "RASTER, NOT COLOUR");
    } else {
        text.print(centered(CAPTION_ROW, 21), "NO RASTER SPLITS HERE");
    }

    // "FRAME 00000": the label once, the counter's cell kept for the loop.
    let cell: usmallint = centered(FRAME_ROW, 11);
    text.print(cell, "FRAME");
    frameCell = cell + 6;

    // What the machine itself declares — the line that separates "still
    // by design" from "broken". Video.RASTER is a bool and printNumber
    // takes a number, so it is widened here; both sides of the `if` are
    // compile-time constants, so this is one literal by the time the
    // backend sees it.
    let answers: utinyint = 0;
    if (Video.RASTER) {
        answers = 1;
    }
    cell = centered(FACT_ROW, 8);
    text.print(cell, "RASTER");
    text.printNumber(cell + 7, answers, 1);
    return;
}

export function main(): void {
    screen.blank();
    Mark();
    audio.play(chime);
    drawScreen();

    if (BANDS) {
        // The list, built once: lines and slots stand for the program's
        // whole life, and only the band's value bytes are rewritten each
        // frame. Entries go in ascending line order — the C64's handler
        // reads the list live and the web enforces the same rule.
        raster.clear();
        raster.at(ABOVE, Slot.BACKGROUND, BAND_BACKGROUND);
        raster.at(ABOVE, Slot.BORDER, BAND_BORDER);
        if (WOBBLES) {
            for (let i: utinyint = 0; i < BAND_ENTRIES; i++) {
                raster.at(BAND_TOP + i * 2, Slot.SCROLL_X, SINE[i]);
            }
            raster.at(STRAIGHT, Slot.SCROLL_X, 0);
            bandFirst = raster.STRIDE * 2; // entry 2: the first band's line
        }
        raster.at(BELOW, Slot.BACKGROUND, 0);
        raster.at(BELOW, Slot.BORDER, 0);

        raster.enable();
    }

    while (true) {
        waitFrame();

        if (WOBBLES) {
            // Rebuild only the band's 12 value bytes, right after
            // waitFrame() — before the raster reaches the first entry's
            // line, per @8bitscript/c64/raster's rebuild rule. SCROLL_X
            // keeps its 0-7 slot semantics through setValue, so the sine
            // value goes in as-is on every machine.
            phase = (phase + 1) & 31;
            for (let i: utinyint = 0; i < BAND_ENTRIES; i++) {
                raster.setValue(bandFirst + i * raster.STRIDE, SINE[(i + phase) & 31]);
            }
        }

        // The liveness signal, every frame. On a machine with no raster
        // this is the only thing on screen that moves.
        frames++;
        text.printNumber(frameCell, frames, 5);
        graphics.update();
        audio.update();
    }
}
